---
layout: article
title: Memory
description: Learn how Appwrite Agent memory stores preferences, instructions, and facts across conversations.
---

**Memory** lets you save durable notes the Agent can reuse across chats. Use it for standing preferences (response style), instructions (how to treat your projects), and facts (stable details about your stack).

Manage memory under **Agent** > **Settings** > **Memory**.

# Categories {% #categories %}

| Category | Use for |
|----------|---------|
| Preference | How you want the Agent to respond (tone, format, verbosity) |
| Instruction | Standing rules the Agent should follow when helping you |
| Fact | Stable information about your apps, orgs, or conventions |

Each memory has a **key**, **content**, optional **priority**, and an active or archived status.

# Priority and status {% #priority-and-status %}

- **Priority** influences which memories are more important when the Agent has a limited context budget. Higher priority memories are preferred when not everything can fit.
- **Active** memories are available to the Agent.
- **Archived** memories stay saved but are not used until you activate them again.

# Scoping {% #scoping %}

Memories belong to your Console account. Depending on how a memory is created, it may be associated with user, team, or project scope so the Agent applies it in the right context. Prefer clear, specific content so the Agent does not over-generalize.

# Good memory examples {% #good-memory-examples %}

- Preference: `Prefer short answers with numbered steps`
- Instruction: `Always use TablesDB terminology, not collections, for this org`
- Fact: `Production project region is fra`

Avoid storing secrets (API keys, passwords, tokens) in memory. Use project settings and secure secret storage instead.

